.TH GPU 3
.SH NAME
gpu \- graphics devices for user-space drivers
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B bind -a #G /dev

.B #G/0/ctl
.B #G/0/irq
.B #G/0/dirty
 ...
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.I gpu
device is the kernel's whole part of a graphics driver: it hands
the hardware of every PCI display controller (class 03) to a
user-space driver, such as
.IR virtiogpu
in
.IR gpu (4).
It knows nothing about mode setting, memory management, command
submission or firmware.
.PP
Each controller has a directory
.IR n ,
numbered from 0 in PCI order, with three files.
.PP
Reading
.B ctl
returns lines of text:
.TF "bar\fIi\fP \fIaddr size seg\fP"
.TP
.BI pci " bus.dev.fn"
the device, as named in
.BR #$/pci ;
its configuration space is
.BI #$/pci/ bus.dev.fn raw\f1.
.TP
.BI id " vid/did
vendor and device id.
.TP
.BI irq " line state count kind
the interrupt line, whether
.B irq
has enabled it, the interrupts seen, and
.B msi
or
.BR intx .
.TP
.BI bar i " addr size seg \fR[\fPcseg wseg\fR]\fP
a base address register: physical address, size and the physical
segment that maps it, uncached (see
.IR segattach (2)),
.B -
for an I/O bar; after
.BI "bar " i " cached"
also the write-back and the write-combining segment.
.TP
.BI mem " addr size seg
memory of the firmware's the driver needs, outside the bars: for an
Intel device the ACPI OpRegion (its address from the device's ASLS
register), which holds the video BIOS tables; a write-back segment.
.TP
.BI fb " addr size seg
the framebuffer, if allocated, else
.BR "fb none" .
.PD
.PP
The memory bars are attachable segments named
.BI gpu n bar i\f1,
uncached; the framebuffer is
.BI gpu n fb\f1.
Only the hostowner may attach them: a GPU reaches all of the
machine's memory (DMA); its driver gives clients views of their own
objects
.RB ( phys
in
.IR segment (3)).
.PP
Writing
.B wbinvd
to
.B ctl
writes back and invalidates the caches of all processors.
.PP
Writing
.B ctl
(by the host owner) controls the device:
.TF "fb \fIbytes\fP"
.TP
.B "bme on\fR|\fPoff
enable or disable bus mastering.
.TP
.BI fb " bytes
allocate a physically contiguous framebuffer of at least
.I bytes
bytes, once; later requests that fit are no-ops.
.TP
.B "irq ack
unmask the interrupt (see below).
.TP
.BI bar " n " cached
also map memory bar
.I n
write-back, as the segment
.BI gpu n bar n c\f1,
and write-combining, as
.BI gpu n bar n w\f1,
and make the MTRRs write-back, so that the page attributes decide;
only for memory that is RAM to the processor, such as virtio-gpu's
host visible memory, which only the driver knows.
The driver tells its clients which part to map how.
.PD
.PP
Opening
.B irq
enables the device's interrupt: MSI where the device has it
(the interrupt line need not be assigned), else legacy INTx.
A read blocks until the device interrupts and returns the count.
With INTx the kernel's handler masks the interrupt with the
interrupt-disable bit of the PCI command register; the driver serves
the device and writes
.B "irq ack
to unmask it (with MSI, an edge, nothing is masked and
.B "irq ack
does no harm).
.PP
.B dirty
connects the framebuffer to
.IR draw (3):
with
.IR vga (3)
type
.BR gpu ,
the screen is the framebuffer of controller 0, drawn into directly,
and every flush of the screen is collected here.
A read blocks until something changed and returns the bounding
rectangle as
.IB "x0 y0 x1 y1" .
Each open file collects for itself (up to four at once): the driver's
copy to the card and a compositor
.RI ( wsys (4)
.BR -g )
each get every change.
The driver copies that part to the display.
.SH EXAMPLES
What the driver does to show the screen:
.EX
	echo fb 9216000 >'#G/0/ctl'
	echo type gpu >/dev/vgactl
	echo size 1280x800x32 x8r8g8b8 >/dev/vgactl
	echo drawinit >/dev/vgactl
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /sys/src/9/port/devgpu.c
.br
.B /sys/src/9/pc/vgagpu.c
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.IR gpu (4),
.IR segment (3),
.IR vga (3),
.IR segattach (2)
.SH BUGS
Only the first controller's framebuffer can be the screen.
.br
Only legacy interrupts; no MSI.
.br
The framebuffer is never freed.
.br
The kernel's console keeps writing on the screen.
